From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:25:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: identity, store and what the transport needs:
 prpl-telegram, log level, cryptofs store, contacts, presence, mute and
 archive

* Register as prpl-telegram

The webOS IM transport (imlibpurpletransport) maps the account template's
type_telegram service to the libpurple protocol id prpl-telegram, the id the
older tgl-based telegram-purple plugin used, and looks the plugin up with
purple_find_prpl() under that name. With the upstream id, telegram-tdlib,
the lookup returns NULL and every Telegram login fails before it starts.

Not for upstream: purple accounts are stored by protocol id, so changing it
would orphan every existing tdlib-purple account, and prpl-telegram is the
id of a different plugin there.

* Cap TDLib's log verbosity at 1

TDLib logs at verbosity 5 unless told otherwise, which writes out every
network query and update. On webOS the plugin runs inside the shared IM
transport, whose output ends up in one log (/media/internal/imstdout.log)
with every other plugin's, and TDLib alone filled it at about 1.5 MB a
minute, burying everything else.

Set the verbosity to 1 (fatal errors and warnings) right before each client
is created. This overrides the level tgprpl_init() derives from libpurple's
debug settings.

Not for upstream: elsewhere that derived level is the right control.

* Keep the TDLib store on /media/cryptofs, and remove it with the account

TDLib's database directory, which is also its files directory, lives under
purple_user_dir(), which on webOS is /var/preferences: a ~62 MB "store-var"
partition. Both the sqlite database (tens of MB on an active account) and
the unbounded media cache (photos, videos, files, stickers) fill it.

Put both on the large /media/cryptofs partition instead, as sibling
per-username directories created up front:
/media/cryptofs/.purple-tdlib-db/<username> and
/media/cryptofs/.purple-tdlib-files/<username>. cryptofs is FUSE-POSIX and
copes with sqlite WAL (WebKit keeps WAL databases there); the vfat
/media/internal must not hold the database, having no reliable fcntl locking
or -shm mmap. use_file_database is turned on with it, so that TDLib keeps
track of the files in that cache across restarts.

Nothing removed that store when an account was deleted, leaving the session
keys and the media cache behind. Hook libpurple's "account-removed" signal
and wipe both directories for the removed account, keyed strictly on its own
username. If the account is deleted while still connecting (stuck at the
code prompt, say), purple_accounts_delete() has not run tgprpl_close() and
TDLib is still writing to the store, so the live client is first destroyed
synchronously (~TdTransceiver sends close and joins the poll thread), with
protocol_data cleared so the later tgprpl_close() does not free it again.
Both sendTdlibParameters() and the teardown build the paths through
PurpleTdClient::getDatabaseDir()/getFilesDir(), so they always agree.

Not for upstream: the paths are those of the webOS partition layout.

* Hand the transport what it needs for contacts and presence

The webOS IM transport turns libpurple buddies into db8 contacts and group
messages into Messaging app rows. Four things it needs that stock
tdlib-purple does not give it:

- Profile fields. Telegram knows a user's phone number, @username and
  first/last name, but tdlib-purple only shows them in the user-info popup.
  Store them on the buddy's blist node (tdlib-phone, tdlib-username,
  tdlib-first-name, tdlib-last-name; the transport reads these keys
  literally), for new and existing buddies alike, removing a key when the
  field is empty.
- Presence. Upstream maps every status other than online to AWAY, which the
  transport shows as an orange "idle" dot on every contact. Map only
  userStatusRecently ("seen within the last couple of days", the usual
  privacy-limited answer) to AWAY, and everything else - offline, last
  week, last month, hidden - to OFFLINE.
- The sender of a group message. serv_got_chat_in() has a single `who`
  slot, which carries the display name; there is no way to reach the
  sender from it. Send "id<userId>\x1f<display name>" instead when the
  sender is a user; the transport splits on \x1f, using the id as the
  routable address (so the app can open a 1:1 with the sender) and the
  rest as the name.
- Handle-style addressing. webOS reduces a contact's display name to a
  handle - ASCII alphanumerics only, lowercased ("Herman van Hazendonk" ->
  "hermanvanhazendonk") - and may address a conversation by it. Resolve
  such names as a last resort, after display name and @username.

The presence mapping and the sender's id are for the Messaging app
only, whose front-end, imlibpurpleservice, gives libpurple the UI name
"Palm Messaging" (isWebosUi()). Other front-ends keep upstream's
mapping and get the sender as before.

Not for upstream: the blist keys, the \x1f convention and the handle
normalization are contracts with the webOS transport.

* Expose mute and archive state, and leave archived chats out

The webOS transport suppresses notifications for conversations the user has
muted, and silences archived ones, by reading two booleans off the blist
node: "muted" and "archived". Keep them in sync with Telegram:

- handle updateChatNotificationSettings (a chat muted or unmuted, here or on
  another client), storing the new settings, and refresh both flags then
  and whenever a chat is (re)processed in updateChat(). A chat counts as
  muted only when it is muted explicitly (mute_for > 0, not deferring to
  the scope default); "mute all groups" style defaults are not considered.
  A chat counts as archived when it has a position in the Archive list and
  none in the Main list.
- leave chats that are only in the Archive list out of the buddy list
  (isChatInContactList), so archived people and groups do not show up as
  webOS contacts.
- add /archive and /unarchive chat commands, which move the current chat
  into or out of the Archive list with addChatToList.

Not for upstream as it is: the blist keys are a contract with the webOS
transport, and hiding archived chats from the buddy list changes what
every other front-end shows. The two commands could be offered on their
own.

* Let the test suite build and log in again

The tests had fallen behind the plug-in, and `tests` no longer linked.

The webOS patches call libpurple functions the test mock does not
have (purple_signal_emit, purple_timeout_add,
purple_blist_node_set_bool, purple_get_core,
purple_accounts_get_handle). Add them to the mock. purple_timeout_add
runs its function once, at once, so the delayed receipt frees what it
holds.

Every test also starts with a login that no longer matched the
plug-in: TDLib's database is now in
/media/cryptofs/.purple-tdlib-db/<number>, not under
purple_user_dir(). Expect it there.

* Send a file by its content

The Messaging app attaches every file by file transfer: it has no message
of its own to write [Voice#N] or [Video#N] placeholders into. Turn on
"send-files-by-content" by default, so a recording goes as a voice
message, a video as a video and a picture as a photo, as 0003 can do;
FileTransferTest.SendFile_ByContent sets it off explicitly for its first
half, and the login-through-chat tests expect the cryptofs store.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
